<hr/>
<ul><li><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_characters_for_transcribing_Slavonic" title="Chinese characters for transcribing Slavonic">Slavonic transcription</a></li></ul></div></div></td>
</tr><tr><td class="sidebar-below hlist">
<ul><li><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transliteration_of_Chinese" title="Transliteration of Chinese">Transliteration of Chinese</a></li></ul></td></tr><tr><td class="sidebar-navbar"><span class="mw-empty-elt"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1374427404" about="#mwt16" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Hlist/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1239400231" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt17" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Navbar/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'>.mw-parser-output .navbar{display:inline;font-size:88%;font-weight:normal}.mw-parser-output .navbar-collapse{float:left;text-align:left}.mw-parser-output .navbar-boxtext{word-spacing:0}.mw-parser-output .navbar ul{display:inline-block;white-space:nowrap;line-height:inherit}.mw-parser-output .navbar-brackets::before{margin-right:-0.125em;content:"[ "}.mw-parser-output .navbar-brackets::after{margin-left:-0.125em;content:" ]"}.mw-parser-output .navbar li{word-spacing:-0.125em}.mw-parser-output .navbar a>span,.mw-parser-output .navbar a>abbr{text-decoration:inherit}.mw-parser-output .navbar-mini abbr{font-variant:small-caps;border-bottom:none;text-decoration:none;cursor:inherit}.mw-parser-output .navbar-ct-full{font-size:114%;margin:0 7em}.mw-parser-output .navbar-ct-mini{font-size:114%;margin:0 4em}html.skin-theme-clientpref-night .mw-parser-output .navbar li a abbr{color:var(--color-base)!important}@media(prefers-color-scheme:dark){html.skin-theme-clientpref-os .mw-parser-output .navbar li a abbr{color:var(--color-base)!important}}@media print{.mw-parser-output .navbar{display:none!important}}</style></span><div class="navbar plainlinks hlist navbar-mini"><ul><li class="nv-view"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Chinese_characters_sidebar" title="Template:Chinese characters sidebar"><abbr title="View this template">v</abbr></a></li><li class="nv-talk"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Chinese_characters_sidebar" title="Template talk:Chinese characters sidebar"><abbr title="Discuss this template">t</abbr></a></li><li class="nv-edit"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Template:Chinese_characters_sidebar" title="Special:EditPage/Template:Chinese characters sidebar"><abbr title="Edit this template">e</abbr></a></li></ul></div></td></tr></tbody></table>
<ul id="mwGA"><li id="mwGQ">Form-based orders, including stroke-based orders and component-based orders, which further includes radical-based orders, etc.</li>
<li id="mwGg">Sound-based orders, including Pinyin-based order and Bopomofo-based order</li>
<li id="mwGw">Meaning-based orders</li></ul>

<p id="mwHA">In modern Chinese, people also use <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_list" title="Word list" id="mwHQ">frequency orders</a>, where words or characters are sorted by their frequencies of use in a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Text_corpus" title="Text corpus" id="mwHg">text corpus</a>. There is also computer-based sorting and lookup.</p>

<p id="mwHw">Chinese dictionaries include character dictionaries (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_language" title="Chinese language" about="#mwt18" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwIA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang-zh","href":"./Template:Lang-zh"},"params":{"p":{"wt":"zìdiǎn"},"c":{"wt":"字典"}},"i":0}}]}'>Chinese</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt18">:</span><span about="#mwt18"> </span><span lang="zh" about="#mwt18"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Chinese-language_text"/>字典</span><span about="#mwt18">; </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinyin" title="Pinyin" about="#mwt18">pinyin</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt18">:</span><span about="#mwt18"> </span><i about="#mwt18" id="mwIQ"><span lang="zh-Latn">zìdiǎn</span></i>)<sup about="#mwt19" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-3"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":" not listing both simplified and traditional Chinese when they are of the same form."}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-3" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwIg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwIw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwJA">[</span>a<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwJQ">]</span></span></a></sup> and word dictionaries (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simplified_Chinese_characters" title="Simplified Chinese characters" about="#mwt22" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwJg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang-zh","href":"./Template:Lang-zh"},"params":{"p":{"wt":"cídiǎn"},"t":{"wt":"詞典"},"s":{"wt":"词典"}},"i":0}}]}'>simplified Chinese</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt22">:</span><span about="#mwt22"> </span><span lang="zh-Hans" about="#mwt22"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_simplified_Chinese-language_text"/>词典</span><span about="#mwt22">; </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traditional_Chinese_characters" title="Traditional Chinese characters" about="#mwt22">traditional Chinese</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt22">:</span><span about="#mwt22"> </span><span lang="zh-Hant" about="#mwt22"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_traditional_Chinese-language_text"/>詞典</span><span about="#mwt22">; </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinyin" title="Pinyin" about="#mwt22">pinyin</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt22">:</span><span about="#mwt22"> </span><i about="#mwt22" id="mwJw"><span lang="zh-Latn">cídiǎn</span></i>). Chinese word orders are based on character orders. Single-character words are arranged by character sorting directly, and multi-character words can be sorted character by character in a similar way.<sup about="#mwt23" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWangZou200362-141_4-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWangZou200362-141"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWangZou200362-141-4"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wang"},"2":{"wt":"Zou"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"62-141"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWangZou200362-141-4" id="mwKA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwKQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwKg">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwKw">]</span></span></a></sup>
In the following sections, there is a general introduction to the orders and sorting methods currently in use, focused on those which are more popular and effective.</p>

<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" id="mwLA" data-mw='{"autoGenerated":true}'/></section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwLQ" aria-labelledby="Form-based_orders"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Form-based_orders">Form-based orders</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1" title="Edit section: Form-based orders"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwLg">In this category of orders, Chinese characters are sorted according to various features of their forms or shapes. There are two subcategories of form-based orders: stroke-based orders and component-based orders.<sup about="#mwt26" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWangZou200320_5-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWangZou200320"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWangZou200320-5"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wang"},"2":{"wt":"Zou"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"20"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWangZou200320-5" id="mwLw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwMA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMQ">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mwMw" aria-labelledby="Stroke-based_orders"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Stroke-based_orders">Stroke-based orders</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2" title="Edit section: Stroke-based orders"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt29" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwNA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Stroke-based sorting"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt30" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt29" id="mwNQ">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stroke-based_sorting" title="Stroke-based sorting">Stroke-based sorting</a></div>
<p id="mwNg">In stroke-based orders, Chinese characters are sorted by different features of strokes, including stroke counts, stroke forms, stroke orders, stroke combinations, stroke positions, etc.<sup about="#mwt31" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014205–207_6-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014205–207"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014205–207-6"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"pp":{"wt":"205-207"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014205–207-6" id="mwNw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwOA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwOQ">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwOg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="3" id="mwOw" aria-labelledby="Stroke-count_order"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Stroke-count_order">Stroke-count order</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3" title="Edit section: Stroke-count order"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwPA">In this order, Chinese characters are sorted by their <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stroke_count" title="Stroke count" class="mw-redirect" id="mwPQ">stroke count</a> ascendingly. A character with less strokes is put before those of more strokes.<sup about="#mwt34" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWangZou200323-24_7-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWangZou200323-24"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWangZou200323-24-7"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wang"},"2":{"wt":"Zou"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"23-24"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWangZou200323-24-7" id="mwPg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwPw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQA">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQQ">]</span></span></a></sup> For example, the different characters in "漢字筆劃, 汉字笔画 " (Chinese character strokes) are sorted into "汉(5)字(6)画(8)笔(10)[筆(12)畫(12)]漢(14)", where stroke counts are put in brackets. (Please note that both 筆 and 畫 are of 12 strokes and their order is not determinable by stroke-count order.).</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="4" id="mwQg" aria-labelledby="Stroke-count-stroke-order_sorting"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Stroke-count-stroke-order_sorting">Stroke-count-stroke-order sorting</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4" title="Edit section: Stroke-count-stroke-order sorting"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwQw">This is a combination of stroke-count sorting and stroke-order sorting.<sup about="#mwt37" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWangZou200324-25_8-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWangZou200324-25"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWangZou200324-25-8"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wang"},"2":{"wt":"Zou"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"24-25"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWangZou200324-25-8" id="mwRA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwRQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRg">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwRw">]</span></span></a></sup> Characters are first arranged by stroke-counts ascendingly. Then Stroke-order sorting is employed to sort characters with the same number of strokes. The characters are first arranged by their first strokes according to an order of stroke groups (such as “<i id="mwSA">heng</i> (横), <i id="mwSQ">shu</i> (竖), <i id="mwSg">pie</i> (撇), <i id="mwSw">dian</i> (点), <i id="mwTA">zhe</i> (折)”, or “<i id="mwTQ">dian</i> (点), <i id="mwTg">heng</i> (横), <i id="mwTw">shu</i> (竖), <i id="mwUA">pie</i> (撇), <i id="mwUQ">zhe</i> (折)”), if the first strokes belong to the same group, then sort by their second strokes in a similar way, and so on.<sup about="#mwt40" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELi2013326-327_9-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELi2013326-327"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELi2013326-327-9"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Li"},"2":{"wt":"2013"},"p":{"wt":"326-327"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELi2013326-327-9" id="mwUg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwUw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVA">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVQ">]</span></span></a></sup> In our example of the previous section, both 筆 and 畫 are of 12 strokes. 筆 starts with stroke ㇓of the <i id="mwVg">pie</i> (撇) group, and 畫 starts with ㇕ of the <i id="mwVw">zhe</i> (折) group, and <i id="mwWA">pie</i> is before <i id="mwWQ">zhe</i> in the groups order, so 筆 comes before 畫. Hence the different characters in "汉字笔画, 漢字筆劃" are finally sorted into "汉(5)字(6)画(8)笔(10)筆(12)畫(12)漢(14)", where each character is put at its unique position.</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="5" id="mwWg" aria-labelledby="GB_stroke-based_order"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="GB_stroke-based_order">GB stroke-based order</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5" title="Edit section: GB stroke-based order"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt43" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwWw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"GB stroke-based order"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt44" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt43" id="mwXA">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GB_stroke-based_order" title="GB stroke-based order">GB stroke-based order</a></div>
<p id="mwXQ">GB Stroke-Based Order, full name <i id="mwXg">GB13000.1 Character Set Chinese Character Order (Stroke-Based Order) (GB13000.1字符集汉字字序（笔画序）规范)</i><sup about="#mwt45" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTENational_Language_Commission_of_China1999_10-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTENational Language Commission of China1999"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTENational_Language_Commission_of_China1999-10"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"National Language Commission of China"},"2":{"wt":"1999"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTENational_Language_Commission_of_China1999-10" id="mwXw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwYA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwYQ">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwYg">]</span></span></a></sup> is a standard released by the National Language Commission of China in 1999. This is an enhanced version of stroke-count-stroke-order sorting. According to this standard, the characters are first sorted by stroke counts, followed by stroke order (of the five families of <i id="mwYw">heng</i>, <i id="mwZA">shu</i>, <i id="mwZQ">pie</i>, <i id="mwZg">dian</i> and <i id="mwZw">zhe</i>). Then if there are characters of the same stroke count and stroke order, they will be sorted by the primary-secondary stroke order. For example, 子 and 孑 are both of 3 strokes and have the same five-group stroke order (㇐ and ㇀ both belong to the <i id="mwaA">heng</i> family), but according to the rule of primary-secondary stroke order, primary stroke ㇐ is before secondary stroke ㇀. So 子 comes before 孑. If two characters are of the same stroke count, stroke order and primary-secondary stroke, then sort them according to the mode of stroke combination. Stroke separation precedes stroke connection, and connection precedes intersection. For example: 八 is before 人, which is before 乂. And there are other sorting rules for more accurate sorting.</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="6" id="mwaQ" aria-labelledby="YES_order"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="YES_order">YES order</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6" title="Edit section: YES order"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt48" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwag" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"YES stroke alphabetical order"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt49" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt48" id="mwaw">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YES_stroke_alphabetical_order" title="YES stroke alphabetical order">YES stroke alphabetical order</a></div>
<p id="mwbA">YES<sup about="#mwt50" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-11" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-11"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"efn","href":"./Template:Efn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":" YES is the acronym of \"Yi Er San\", the pinyin of \"一二三\", which is the Chinese name of the sorting method."}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-11" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwbQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwbg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwbw">[</span>b<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcA">]</span></span></a></sup> is a simplified stroke-based sorting method free from stroke counting and grouping, without compromising accuracy. And it has been successfully applied to the indexing of all the characters in the <i id="mwcQ"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xinhua_Zidian" title="Xinhua Zidian" class="mw-redirect" id="mwcg">Xinhua Zidian</a></i> and <i id="mwcw"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiandai_Hanyu_Cidian" title="Xiandai Hanyu Cidian" id="mwdA">Xiandai Hanyu Cidian</a></i>. In this joint index you can look up a Chinese character to find its pinyin and Unicode, in addition to the page numbers in the two popular dictionaries.<sup about="#mwt53" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZhang2013_12-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZhang2013"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZhang2013-12"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zhang"},"2":{"wt":"2013"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZhang2013-12" id="mwdQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwdg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwdw">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mweA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="7" id="mweQ" aria-labelledby="Component-based_orders"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Component-based_orders">Component-based orders</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7" title="Edit section: Component-based orders"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mweg">In this category, characters are sorted by one or more components.</p>

<section data-mw-section-id="8" id="mwew" aria-labelledby="Radical-based_orders"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Radical-based_orders">Radical-based orders</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8" title="Edit section: Radical-based orders"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwfA">A <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radical_(Chinese_characters)" title="Radical (Chinese characters)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwfQ">radical</a> (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_language" title="Chinese language" about="#mwt56" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwfg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang-zh","href":"./Template:Lang-zh"},"params":{"p":{"wt":"bùshǒu"},"c":{"wt":"部首"},"l":{"wt":"section head"}},"i":0}}]}'>Chinese</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt56">:</span><span about="#mwt56"> </span><span lang="zh" about="#mwt56"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Chinese-language_text"/>部首</span><span about="#mwt56">; </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinyin" title="Pinyin" about="#mwt56">pinyin</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt56">:</span><span about="#mwt56"> </span><i about="#mwt56"><span lang="zh-Latn">bùshǒu</span></i><span about="#mwt56">; </span><abbr title="Literal translation" about="#mwt56"><small>lit.</small></abbr><span about="#mwt56"> </span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt56">'</span><span about="#mwt56">section head</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt56">'</span>) is a common component shared by a group of characters. The radical usually lies on the upper part or left side of a character and helps to express its meaning.<sup about="#mwt57" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014190-191_13-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014190-191"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014190-191-13"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"p":{"wt":"190-191"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014190-191-13" id="mwfw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwgA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwgQ">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwgg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt60" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEQiu2013102–108_14-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEQiu2013102–108"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEQiu2013102–108-14"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Qiu"},"2":{"wt":"2013"},"pp":{"wt":"102-108"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEQiu2013102–108-14" id="mwgw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwhA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhQ">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhg">]</span></span></a></sup> For example, 花 (flower), 草 ( grass), 菜 (vegetable) all have the radical of 艹 (艸, 草, glass/plant), which indicates they are related to plant; 推 (push), 拉 (pull), 打(beat) share the radical of 扌 (手, hand), and are actions normally involving hands. In radical-based order, all the characters sharing a radical are put under that radical to form a radical family or section. Different families are arranged by their leading radicals in stroke-based order, and characters inside a family are also sorted by their strokes.<sup about="#mwt63" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELi2013324_15-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELi2013324"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELi2013324-15"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Li"},"2":{"wt":"2013"},"p":{"wt":"324"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELi2013324-15" id="mwhw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwiA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwiQ">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwig">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwiw">In many contemporary dictionaries, including <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xinhua_Zidian" title="Xinhua Zidian" class="mw-redirect" id="mwjA">Xinhua Zidian</a>, Xiandai Hanyu Cidian and Oxford Chinese Dictionary,<sup about="#mwt66" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKleeman2010_16-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKleeman2010"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKleeman2010-16"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Kleeman"},"2":{"wt":"2010"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKleeman2010-16" id="mwjQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwjg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjw">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkA">]</span></span></a></sup> the radical-based character lookup system consists of three indexes or tables: a radical index, a character lookup index, and an index of characters with radicals difficult to find, all sorted in stroke-based order. To lookup a character (such as 家, home) in a dictionary (e.g., Xinhua Zidian, version 12<sup about="#mwt69" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELanguage_Institute2020_17-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELanguage Institute2020"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELanguage_Institute2020-17"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Language Institute"},"2":{"wt":"2020"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELanguage_Institute2020-17" id="mwkQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwkg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkw">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwlA">]</span></span></a></sup>), first find out its radical (the component 宀 at the top of 家). Count its number of strokes (3 strokes in 宀) and find it in the radical index in stroke-based order. When found, get its page number (p49) on the right side. Then, according to the page number, find the radical family in the character lookup table in stroke-based order. Count the number of strokes in the remaining parts of the character (except radical 宀, there are 7 strokes in 家) and find the target character within the family. And the page number on the right (217) is the page number in the dictionary main body for the entry of the character (characters entries in the main body of Xinhua Zidian are sorted by Pinyin). Characters with radicals difficult to find out can be looked up in the Index of Characters with Radicals Difficult to Find in stroke-based order.</p>

<p id="mwlQ">The first radical system in history was created by a Chinese Scholar <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xu_Shen" title="Xu Shen" id="mwlg">Xu Shen</a> in his <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuowen_Jiezi" title="Shuowen Jiezi" id="mwlw">Shuowen Jiezi</a> dictionary almost two thousand years ago in the Eastern Han Dynasty. This dictionary is still available today, with a total number of 540 radicals. Another milestone is the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kangxi_radical" title="Kangxi radical" class="mw-redirect" id="mwmA">Kangxi radical</a> system employed in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kangxi_Dictionary" title="Kangxi Dictionary" id="mwmQ">Kangxi Dictionary</a> in 1716 in the era of Emperor Kangxi, with the number of radicals reduced to 214.<sup about="#mwt72" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014185–188_18-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014185–188"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014185–188-18"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"pp":{"wt":"185-188"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014185–188-18" id="mwmg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwmw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwnA">[</span>16<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwnQ">]</span></span></a></sup> The Kangxi radical sorting method is still in use in China, Japan and Korea. It is also used by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unicode_collation_algorithm" title="Unicode collation algorithm" id="mwng">Unicode collation algorithm</a> to sort <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CJK_Unified_Ideographs" title="CJK Unified Ideographs" id="mwnw">CJK Unified Ideographs</a>. The latest standard radical table of Chinese Mainland is the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Table_of_Indexing_Chinese_Character_Components" title="Table of Indexing Chinese Character Components" id="mwoA">Table of Indexing Chinese Character Components</a> with a list of 201 radicals.<sup about="#mwt75" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTECommercial_Press2021225–234_19-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTECommercial Press2021225–234"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTECommercial_Press2021225–234-19"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Commercial Press"},"2":{"wt":"2021"},"pp":{"wt":"225-234"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTECommercial_Press2021225–234-19" id="mwoQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwog"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwow">[</span>17<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwpA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="9" id="mwpQ" aria-labelledby="Four-corner_order"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Four-corner_order">Four-corner order</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9" title="Edit section: Four-corner order"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwpg">Chinese characters are written in the form of a square block. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four-Corner_Method" title="Four-Corner Method" class="mw-redirect" id="mwpw">Four-Corner Method</a> assigns a 4-digit code to a character, each digit representing one corner of the block. The four corner digits appear in the sequence of "upper-left, upper-right, lower-left and lower-right". For example, the code of character 顏 (meaning "face") is 0128, where the first digit 0 represents the upper-left component 亠, 1 for the upper right 一， 2 for the lower-left ㇓, and 8 represents the lower-right 八.<sup about="#mwt78" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014203_20-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014203"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014203-20"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"pp":{"wt":"203"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014203-20" id="mwqA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwqQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqg">[</span>18<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwrA">A fifth digit can be added to represent an extra part above the lower-right corner to gain higher sorting accuracy. For example, the extended code of character 佳 is 24214, where the fifth digit 4 represents component 十 above the final 一 in the lower-right corner.<sup about="#mwt81" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014203_20-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014203"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014203-20"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"pp":{"wt":"203"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014203-20" id="mwrQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwrg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwrw">[</span>18<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwsA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwsQ">When a set of characters are encoded in four-corner codes, they are sorted ascendingly into a four-corner order by the first four digits (followed by the fifth digits if they exist).<sup about="#mwt84" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014203–205_21-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014203–205"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014203–205-21"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"pp":{"wt":"203-205"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014203–205-21" id="mwsg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwsw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtA">[</span>19<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="10" id="mwtg" aria-labelledby="Cangjie-code_order"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Cangjie-code_order">Cangjie-code order</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10" title="Edit section: Cangjie-code order"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwtw">In this method, Chinese characters are arranged alphabetically by their codes used in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cangjie_input_method" title="Cangjie input method" id="mwuA">Cangjie input method</a>. The Cangjie code of a character is a string of English letters each representing a selected Cangjie component in the character. For example, the Cangjie codes of the characters in 漢字排檢法 (Methods for Chinese character sorting and retrieving) are 漢(ETLO)字(JND)排(QLMY)檢(DOMO)法(EGI), and can be sorted into a Cangjie-code order of 檢(DOMO)法(EGI)漢(ETLO)字(JND)排(QLMY).<sup about="#mwt90" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-22" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-22"}}'><a href="#cite_note-22" id="mwuQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwug"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwuw">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwvA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwvQ">Compared with sound-based orders, form-based orders are usually more complicated, but have the advantages of (a) allowing character and word lookup without knowing its pronunciation, and (b) effective collation of large character sets without support from other sorting methods.<sup about="#mwt91" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZhan200819-24_23-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZhan200819-24"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZhan200819-24-23"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zhan"},"2":{"wt":"2008"},"p":{"wt":"19-24"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZhan200819-24-23" id="mwvg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwvw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwwA">[</span>21<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwwQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section></section><section data-mw-section-id="11" id="mwwg" aria-labelledby="Sound-based_orders"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Sound-based_orders">Sound-based orders</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11" title="Edit section: Sound-based orders"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwww">There are two sound representation systems currently in use for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_Chinese" title="Standard Chinese" id="mwxA">Standard Chinese</a>, i.e., <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinyin" title="Pinyin" id="mwxQ">pinyin</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bopomofo" title="Bopomofo" id="mwxg">bopomofo</a>. Accordingly, we have two methods of sound-based sorting for Standard Chinese.<sup about="#mwt94" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014197–202_24-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014197–202"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014197–202-24"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"pp":{"wt":"197-202"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014197–202-24" id="mwxw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwyA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwyQ">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwyg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="12" id="mwyw" aria-labelledby="Pinyin-based_order"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Pinyin-based_order">Pinyin-based order</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12" title="Edit section: Pinyin-based order"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt97" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwzA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Pinyin alphabetical order"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt98" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt97" id="mwzQ">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinyin_alphabetical_order" title="Pinyin alphabetical order">Pinyin alphabetical order</a></div>

<p id="mwzg">In this method, Chinese characters are sorted by their Pinyin (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_language" title="Chinese language" about="#mwt99" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwzw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"lang-zh","href":"./Template:Lang-zh"},"params":{"p":{"wt":"pīnyīn"},"c":{"wt":"拼音"}},"i":0}}]}'>Chinese</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt99">:</span><span about="#mwt99"> </span><span lang="zh" about="#mwt99"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_Chinese-language_text"/>拼音</span><span about="#mwt99">; </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinyin" title="Pinyin" about="#mwt99">pinyin</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt99">:</span><span about="#mwt99"> </span><i about="#mwt99" id="mw0A"><span lang="zh-Latn">pīnyīn</span></i>) alphabetically, for example, 汉字拼音排序法 (the Pinyin sorting method of Chinese characters) is sorted into "法(fǎ)汉(hàn)排(pái)拼(pīn)序(xù)音(yīn)字(zì)" with pinyin in brackets. Pinyin expressions of similar letters are ordered by their tones in the order of "tone 1, tone 2, tone 3, tone 4 and tone 5 (light tone)", such as "妈(mā), 麻(má), 马(mǎ), 骂(mà), 吗(ma)". Characters of the same sound, i.e., same Pinyin letters and tones, are normally sorted by a stroke-based method.<sup about="#mwt100" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014200–201_25-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014200–201"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014200–201-25"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"pp":{"wt":"200-201"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014200–201-25" id="mw0Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw0g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw0w">[</span>23<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw1A">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw1Q">Words of multiple characters can be sorted in two different ways.<sup about="#mwt103" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014201–202_26-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014201–202"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014201–202-26"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"pp":{"wt":"201-202"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014201–202-26" id="mw1g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw1w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw2A">[</span>24<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw2Q">]</span></span></a></sup> One is to sort character by character. If the first characters are the same, then sort by the second character, and so on. For example, "归并 (guībìng), 归还 (guīhuán), 规划 (guīhuà), 鬼话(guǐhuà), 桂花 (guìhuā)". This method is used in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiandai_Hanyu_Cidian" title="Xiandai Hanyu Cidian" id="mw2g">Xiandai Hanyu Cidian</a>. Another method is to sort according to the pinyin letters of the whole words, followed by sorting on tones when word pinyin letters are the same. For example, "归并 (guībìng), 规划 (guīhuà), 鬼话 (guǐhuà), 桂花 (guìhuā), 归还 (guīhuán)". This method is used in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_Chinese–English_Dictionary" title="ABC Chinese–English Dictionary" id="mw2w">ABC Chinese–English Dictionary</a>.<sup about="#mwt106" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEDeFrancis2003_27-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEDeFrancis2003"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEDeFrancis2003-27"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"DeFrancis"},"2":{"wt":"2003"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDeFrancis2003-27" id="mw3A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw3Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw3g">[</span>25<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw3w">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw4A">Pinyin-based sorting is very convenient for looking up characters or words of which you know their pronunciation and Pinyin expressions. But you can not find words of which you do not know the sound.<sup about="#mwt109" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEZhan200825_28-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEZhan200825"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEZhan200825-28"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Zhan"},"2":{"wt":"2008"},"p":{"wt":"25"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEZhan200825-28" id="mw4Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw4g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw4w">[</span>26<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5A">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="13" id="mw5Q" aria-labelledby="Bopomofo-based_order"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Bopomofo-based_order">Bopomofo-based order</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13" title="Edit section: Bopomofo-based order"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mw5g"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bopomofo" title="Bopomofo" id="mw5w">Bopomofo</a> is a Chinese phonetic system created by the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commission_on_the_Unification_of_Pronunciation" title="Commission on the Unification of Pronunciation" id="mw6A">Commission on the Unification of Pronunciation</a> in 1913, and formally issued by the Ministry of Education of the Chinese Government in 1918. It consists of a table (or alphabet) of letters or symbols in the order of</p>
<pre id="mw6Q">ㄅㄆㄇㄈㄉㄊㄋㄌㄍㄎㄏㄐㄑㄒㄓㄔㄕㄖㄗㄘㄙㄚㄛㄜㄝㄞㄟㄠㄡㄢㄣㄤㄥㄦㄧㄨㄩ </pre>
<p id="mw6g">and 5 tone diacritics of “ˉ, ˊ, ˇ, ˋ, ˙”.<sup about="#mwt112" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014197–200_29-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014197–200"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014197–200-29"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"pp":{"wt":"197-200"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014197–200-29" id="mw6w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw7A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw7Q">[</span>27<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw7g">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt115" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEArcodiaBasciano202183–84_30-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEArcodiaBasciano202183–84"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEArcodiaBasciano202183–84-30"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Arcodia"},"2":{"wt":"Basciano"},"3":{"wt":"2021"},"pp":{"wt":"83-84"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEArcodiaBasciano202183–84-30" id="mw7w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw8A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8Q">[</span>28<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8g">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw8w">Chinese characters are sorted according to the Bopomofo expressions of their sounds by their order in the alphabet table, first by letters, then by tones in the order of "first tone, second tone, third tone, fourth tone, and fifth tone (also called neutral tone, light tone)". For example, the Bopomofo order for the characters in “注音字母排序法 (Bopomofo-based sorting)” is “排(ㄆㄞˊ)母(ㄇㄨˇ)法(ㄈㄚˇ)序(ㄒㄩˋ)注(ㄓㄨˋ)字(ㄗˋ)音 (ㄧㄣ)”. Characters of the same sounds are normally sorted by a stroke-based method.<sup about="#mwt118" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014197–200_29-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014197–200"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014197–200-29"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"pp":{"wt":"197-200"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014197–200-29" id="mw9A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw9Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw9g">[</span>27<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw9w">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw-A">The first dictionary sorted in Bopomofo is 國語辭典 (Guoyu Dictionary) published in 1937,<sup about="#mwt121" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChinese_Dictionary_Compilation_Office1937_31-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEChinese Dictionary Compilation Office1937"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEChinese_Dictionary_Compilation_Office1937-31"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Chinese Dictionary Compilation Office"},"2":{"wt":"1937"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChinese_Dictionary_Compilation_Office1937-31" id="mw-Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw-g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw-w">[</span>29<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw_A">]</span></span></a></sup> followed by many other dictionaries. Bopomofo is more popular in Taiwan than in Chinese Mainland, where Pinyin is predominant.<sup about="#mwt124" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEArcodiaBasciano202183_32-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEArcodiaBasciano202183"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEArcodiaBasciano202183-32"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Arcodia"},"2":{"wt":"Basciano"},"3":{"wt":"2021"},"pp":{"wt":"83"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEArcodiaBasciano202183-32" id="mw_Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw_g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw_w">[</span>30<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="14" id="mwAQE" aria-labelledby="Dialect-sound_orders"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Dialect-sound_orders">Dialect-sound orders</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14" title="Edit section: Dialect-sound orders"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwAQI">In addition to the sounds of standard Chinese, Chinese characters can be sorted alphabetically by the sounds of dialects as well.</p>

<p id="mwAQM">In <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jyutping" title="Jyutping" id="mwAQQ">Jyutping</a> for the Cantonese dialect popular in Hong Kong, the sound of a character is represented by a string of English letters, followed by a number (1 through 6) to represent the tone. For instance, the Jyutping order of the characters in “粵拼排檢法 (Jyutping-based sorting and retrieving)” is “法[faat3]檢[gim2]粵[jyut6]排[paai4]拼[ping3]”, where Jyutping expressions are in square brackets”.<sup about="#mwt127" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELSHK1997_33-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELSHK1997"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELSHK1997-33"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"LSHK"},"2":{"wt":"1997"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELSHK1997-33" id="mwAQU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQc">[</span>31<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAQk">The most serious limitation of sound-based orders is their lack of support to look up words with unknown pronunciation. Hence dictionaries collated by sounds often provide some indexes in form-based orders.<sup about="#mwt130" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWangZou200327_34-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEWangZou200327"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEWangZou200327-34"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wang"},"2":{"wt":"Zou"},"3":{"wt":"2003"},"p":{"wt":"27"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWangZou200327-34" id="mwAQo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQw">[</span>32<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQ0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="15" id="mwAQ4" aria-labelledby="Meaning-based_orders"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Meaning-based_orders">Meaning-based orders</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=15" title="Edit section: Meaning-based orders"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>

<p id="mwAQ8">Meaning-based orders, also called semantics-based orders, arrange characters and words in a hierarchical structure of semantic categories.
The first surviving Chinese dictionary <i id="mwARA"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erya" title="Erya" id="mwARE">Erya</a></i> (date from the 3rd century BC) is arranged by semantic classification. The words were divided into nine categories, each with a large number of entries. An entry is a list of synonyms, which are explained by a word commonly used. For instance, entry</p>
<pre id="mwARI">林、烝、天、地、皇、王、後、辟、公、侯，君也。</pre>
<p id="mwARM">where the ending "君也" means (the previous words are) synonyms of "君 (king)".<sup about="#mwt133" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014184_35-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014184"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014184-35"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"p":{"wt":"184"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014184-35" id="mwARQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARY">[</span>33<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwARg">Modern semantically sorted dictionaries include "同义词词林"<sup about="#mwt136" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMei1996_36-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMei1996"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMei1996-36"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Mei"},"2":{"wt":"1996"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMei1996-36" id="mwARk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARs">[</span>34<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARw">]</span></span></a></sup> and "实用广州话分类词典".<sup about="#mwt139" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMai1997_37-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEMai1997"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEMai1997-37"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Mai"},"2":{"wt":"1997"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMai1997-37" id="mwAR0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAR4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAR8">[</span>35<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASA">]</span></span></a></sup> Their classification systems are much more accurate and detailed than the ancient dictionaries, but still need indexes of radicals or strokes. That means meaning-based sorting is not powerful enough to function as an independent sorting method.<sup about="#mwt142" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014185_38-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014185"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014185-38"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"p":{"wt":"185"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014185-38" id="mwASE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASM">[</span>36<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwASU">Semantics-based sorting involves these questions: What are the categories and subcategories to use? How to put a word into its category and subcategory? How to arrange the categories and subcategories in order? How to arrange the words in the lowest subcategories in order? And the answers to these questions may vary between the user and compiler of the dictionary, and that will lead to difficulties in word lookup.</p>

<p id="mwASY">In fact, radical-based sorting is meaning-based to a certain degree, because in many cases the radical represents the semantic category of a character, e.g., radical 氵(water) in character 江(river), 扌(hand) in 推 (push) and 艹 (grass) in 花草 (flowers and grasses).<sup about="#mwt145" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014190_39-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu2014190"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014190-39"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"p":{"wt":"190"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014190-39" id="mwASc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASk">[</span>37<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt148" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEQiu2013102–103_40-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEQiu2013102–103"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEQiu2013102–103-40"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Qiu"},"2":{"wt":"2013"},"pp":{"wt":"102-103"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEQiu2013102–103-40" id="mwASs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAS0">[</span>38<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAS4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="16" id="mwAS8" aria-labelledby="Frequency-based_orders"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Frequency-based_orders">Frequency-based orders</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=16" title="Edit section: Frequency-based orders"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>

<p id="mwATA">This category of orders have Chinese characters sorted by their frequencies of use, normally in descending order. That means the most frequently used character is at the top of the list. A frequency list is created from a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Text_corpus" title="Text corpus" id="mwATE">text corpus</a>. In <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corpus_linguistics" title="Corpus linguistics" id="mwATI">corpus linguistics</a>, the frequency of a character is the ratio percentage of its number of occurrences in the corpus to the total number of characters of the corpus.<sup about="#mwt151" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu201434_41-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu201434"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu201434-41"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"p":{"wt":"34"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu201434-41" id="mwATM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATU">[</span>39<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwATc">The first frequency list of Chinese characters based on a corpus was created by Chen Heqin (陳鶴琴).<sup about="#mwt154" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu201435_42-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTESu201435"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu201435-42"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Su"},"2":{"wt":"2014"},"p":{"wt":"35"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu201435-42" id="mwATg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATo">[</span>40<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATs">]</span></span></a></sup> In the 1920s, he and his assistants spent two years manually counting the characters in a corpus of 554,478 characters, and obtained 4,261 different characters with frequency information.<sup about="#mwt157" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChen1928_43-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEChen1928"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEChen1928-43"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Chen"},"2":{"wt":"1928"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChen1928-43" id="mwATw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAT0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAT4">[</span>41<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAT8">]</span></span></a></sup>
The top 10 characters in their frequency list are (in descending order):</p>
<pre id="mwAUA">的(of), 不(no, not), 一(one, a/an), 了(had, done), 是 (be), 我(I, me), 上(on, up), 他(he, him), 有(have, has), 人(person, people).</pre>

<p id="mwAUE">In 2001, the Chinese University of Hong Kong published a number of frequency lists on the Web,<sup about="#mwt162" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-44" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-44"}}'><a href="#cite_note-44" id="mwAUI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUQ">[</span>42<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUU">]</span></span></a></sup> entitled "Hong Kong, Mainland China and Taiwan Chinese Frequency: a trans-regional diachronic survey". The frequency data came from a grand corpus with a number of sub-corpora representing the Chinese languages in the three regions of Hong Kong, mainland China and Taiwan and in the two time periods of the 1960s and 1980/1990's. Each sub-corpus includes about 5,000 different characters, as shown by their frequency lists. The top 10 characters in the frequency lists for the three regions of the 1980/1990's are</p>
<pre id="mwAUY">Hong Kong: 的，一，是，不，人，有，在，了，我，中;
Taiwan:    的，一，是，不，人，在，有，我，了，中;
Mainland:  的，一，是，了，不，在，有，人，我，他.</pre>
<p id="mwAUc">As a matter of fact, both meaning-based sorting and frequency-based sorting are employed in other languages as well, though often at word level, not at character or letter level.</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="17" id="mwAUg" aria-labelledby="Orders_of_words"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Orders_of_words">Orders of words</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=17" title="Edit section: Orders of words"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>

<p id="mwAUk">A Chinese word consists of one or more characters. Single-character words can be sorted by a character order, and multi-character words can be sorted character by character in a similar way. For example, according to the Pinyin, Radical and Stroke-based orders used in the <i id="mwAUo"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiandai_Hanyu_Cidian" title="Xiandai Hanyu Cidian" id="mwAUs">Xiandai Hanyu Cidian</a></i> (version 7),<sup about="#mwt163" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELanguage_Institute2016_45-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELanguage Institute2016"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELanguage_Institute2016-45"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Language Institute"},"2":{"wt":"2016"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELanguage_Institute2016-45" id="mwAUw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAU0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAU4">[</span>43<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAU8">]</span></span></a></sup> the five words of [爱, 好, 好事, 好人, 好人家] are arranged in the following orders:</p>
<ul id="mwAVA"><li id="mwAVE">Pinyin-based order: "爱(ài), 好(hǎo), 好人(hǎorén), 好人家(hǎorénjiā), 好事(hǎoshì)".</li>
<li id="mwAVI">Radical-based order: "好(radical 女 of 3 strokes), 好事(事: radical 一 of 1 stroke), 好人(人: radical 人 of 2 strokes), 好人家, 爱(radical 爪 of 4 strokes)".</li>
<li id="mwAVM">Stroke-based order: "好(6 strokes), 好人(人：2 strokes), 好人家, 好事(事：8 strokes), 爱(10 strokes)".</li></ul>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="18" id="mwAVQ" aria-labelledby="Computer_applications"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Computer_applications">Computer applications</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=18" title="Edit section: Computer applications"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwAVU">There are software applications to support sorting and lookup of Chinese characters on the computer.</p>

<section data-mw-section-id="19" id="mwAVY" aria-labelledby="Computer_sorting"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Computer_sorting">Computer sorting</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=19" title="Edit section: Computer sorting"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwAVc">Chinese characters can be automatically sorted on the computer. For example, on Microsoft Windows and Office,<sup about="#mwt168" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-46" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-46"}}'><a href="#cite_note-46" id="mwAVg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVo">[</span>44<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVs">]</span></span></a></sup> users can sort their Chinese texts in the orders of:</p>
<ul id="mwAVw"><li id="mwAV0">Unicode order. (This is primarily Kangxi Radical order.)</li>
<li id="mwAV4">Pinyin order. (More popular in Chinese Mainland)</li>
<li id="mwAV8">Bopomofo order. (More popular in Taiwan)</li>
<li id="mwAWA">Stroke-based order. (More widely used in Hong Kong)</li></ul>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="20" id="mwAWE" aria-labelledby="Computer_word_lookup"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Computer_word_lookup">Computer word lookup</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=20" title="Edit section: Computer word lookup"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwAWI">Here are two examples.</p>

<section data-mw-section-id="21" id="mwAWM" aria-labelledby="The_Unihan_radical-stroke_lookup"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="The_Unihan_radical-stroke_lookup">The Unihan radical-stroke lookup</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=21" title="Edit section: The Unihan radical-stroke lookup"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwAWQ">Unihan Radical-Stroke Index<sup about="#mwt171" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-47" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-47"}}'><a href="#cite_note-47" id="mwAWU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWc">[</span>45<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWg">]</span></span></a></sup> uses the Kangxi radical system. It allows the user to lookup a character from the Unihan Database of more than 75,000 CJK characters,<sup about="#mwt174" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-48" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-48"}}'><a href="#cite_note-48" id="mwAWk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWs">[</span>46<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWw">]</span></span></a></sup> by the procedure of</p>
<ol id="mwAW0"><li id="mwAW4">Input the stroke count of the radical of the target character,</li>
<li id="mwAW8">Among the radicals of that stroke count, select the radical,</li>
<li id="mwAXA">Input the number of additional strokes, and click "Submit",</li>
<li id="mwAXE">Find your character in the returned candidates.</li></ol>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="22" id="mwAXI" aria-labelledby="The_Xinhua_Zidian_mobile_application"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="The_Xinhua_Zidian_mobile_application">The <i id="mwAXM">Xinhua Zidian</i> mobile application</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=22" title="Edit section: The Xinhua Zidian mobile application"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwAXQ">The latest (i.e., 12th) edition of <i id="mwAXU"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xinhua_Zidian" title="Xinhua Zidian" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAXY">Xinhua Zidian</a></i><sup about="#mwt175" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTELanguage_Institute2020_17-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTELanguage Institute2020"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTELanguage_Institute2020-17"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Language Institute"},"2":{"wt":"2020"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTELanguage_Institute2020-17" id="mwAXc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXk">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXo">]</span></span></a></sup> has an accompanying app which supports 3 lookup methods: </p>
<ul id="mwAXs"><li id="mwAXw">Pinyin: The user can input the pinyin letter string and select the target character among the homophones returned by the computer.</li>
<li id="mwAX0">Radical: Input the stroke count of the radical, select the target radical among the radicals of that stroke count, input the number of additional strokes, and pick up the target character among the candidates.</li>
<li id="mwAX4">Stroke: Input the stroke count of the target character, input the first two strokes (in the <i id="mwAX8">heng-shu-pie-dian-zhe</i> groups), then select the target character among the candidates.</li></ul>

</section></section></section><section data-mw-section-id="23" id="mwAYA" aria-labelledby="See_also"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="See_also">See also</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=23" title="Edit section: See also"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<ul id="mwAYE"><li id="mwAYI"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_dictionary" title="Chinese dictionary" id="mwAYM">Chinese dictionary</a></li>
<li id="mwAYQ"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collation" title="Collation" id="mwAYU">Collation</a></li>
<li id="mwAYY"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stroke_order" title="Stroke order" id="mwAYc">Stroke order</a></li>
<li id="mwAYg"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modern_Chinese_characters" title="Modern Chinese characters" id="mwAYk">Modern Chinese characters</a></li>
<li id="mwAYo"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_characters" title="Chinese characters" id="mwAYs">Chinese characters</a></li></ul>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="24" id="mwAYw" aria-labelledby="Notes"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Notes">Notes</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=24" title="Edit section: Notes"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt178" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAY0" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Notelist","href":"./Template:Notelist"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1327269900" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt179" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Reflist/styles.css"}}'>.mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-2{column-width:30em}.mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-3{column-width:25em}body.skin-vector-2022 .mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-2{column-width:27em}body.skin-vector-2022 .mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-3{column-width:22.5em}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=upper-alpha]{list-style-type:upper-alpha}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=upper-roman]{list-style-type:upper-roman}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=lower-alpha]{list-style-type:lower-alpha}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=lower-greek]{list-style-type:lower-greek}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=lower-roman]{list-style-type:lower-roman}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-upper-alpha .references{list-style-type:upper-alpha}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-upper-roman .references{list-style-type:upper-roman}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-lower-alpha .references{list-style-type:lower-alpha}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-lower-greek .references{list-style-type:lower-greek}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-lower-roman .references{list-style-type:lower-roman}</style></span><div about="#mwt178" id="mwAY4">
<div class="mw-references-wrap" typeof="mw:Extension/references" about="#mwt180" id="mwAY8" data-mw='{"name":"references","attrs":{"group":"lower-alpha","responsive":"1"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'><ol class="mw-references references" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" id="mwAZA"><li about="#cite_note-3" id="cite_note-3" data-mw-footnote-number="a"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwAZE"><a href="#cite_ref-3" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwAZI"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwAZM">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-3" class="mw-reference-text reference-text" data-mw-group="lower-alpha"> not listing both simplified and traditional Chinese when they are of the same form.</span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-11" id="cite_note-11" data-mw-footnote-number="b"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwAZQ"><a href="#cite_ref-11" data-mw-group="lower-alpha" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwAZU"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwAZY">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-11" class="mw-reference-text reference-text" data-mw-group="lower-alpha"> YES is the acronym of "Yi Er San", the pinyin of "一二三", which is the Chinese name of the sorting method.</span></li>
</ol></div></div>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="25" id="mwAZc" aria-labelledby="References"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="References">References</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=25" title="Edit section: References"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>

<section data-mw-section-id="26" id="mwAZg" aria-labelledby="Citations"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Citations">Citations</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Chinese_character_orders&amp;action=edit&amp;section=26" title="Edit section: Citations"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt181" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAZk" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Reflist","href":"./Template:Reflist"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1327269900" about="#mwt182" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Reflist/styles.css"}}'/></span><div about="#mwt181" id="mwAZo">
<div class="mw-references-wrap mw-references-columns" typeof="mw:Extension/references" about="#mwt183" id="mwAZs" data-mw='{"name":"references","attrs":{"group":"","responsive":"1"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'><ol class="mw-references references" id="mwAZw"><li about="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEYang2008199-1" id="cite_note-FOOTNOTEYang2008199-1" data-mw-footnote-number="1"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwAZ0"><a href="#cite_ref-FOOTNOTEYang2008199_1-0" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwAZ4"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwAZ8">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEYang2008199-1" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="#CITEREFYang2008" class="mw-selflink-fragment" id="mwAaA">Yang 2008</a>, p.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAaE"> </span>199.</span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014183-2" id="cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014183-2" data-mw-footnote-number="2"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwAaI"><a href="#cite_ref-FOOTNOTESu2014183_2-0" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwAaM"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwAaQ">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTESu2014183-2" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="#CITEREFSu2014" class="mw-selflink-fragment" id="mwAaU">Su 2014</a>, p.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAaY"> </span>183.</span></li>
